  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nHigh pressure has strengthened after the weak trough passed through \nlast Wed afternoon/evening. A very unstable air mass over eastern \nIdaho along with good amounts of moisture as evidenced by the \ndewpoints above 50 deg F. This should trigger a penultimate round of \nthunderstorms for the remainder of the afternoon and into the \nevening tonight. However, a shift in the upper level wind to a more \nwesterly direction will cut off the supply of this unstable moist \nair and by Sat the risk of any precipitation anywhere in the \nforecast area drops to zero and stays that way through at least next \nThu. \n\nLack of clouds will allow hit temperatures to return by Sat, with \nforecasts of at least the upper 90s for elevations below 5500ft and \nsome places exceeding 100 deg F on Sat. There is a slight cool down \non Sun as a dry cold front centered in western Canada moves through \neastern Idaho longitudes. The main effect will be to increase the \nwind for Sat and Sun. This should mean critical fire weather issues \ndiscussed below.\n\nAfter the Canadian front departs the west, the upper level flow \ncontinues out of the west and northwest. This means much drier air \nand an end to thunderstorm activity. The cooling trend also \ncontinues into Mon afternoon as even Snake River plain temperatures \nonly warm into the 80s. The west and northwest flow slows the \nwarming, but by Wed temperatures will be back above 90 for the lower \nelevations. High pressure will keep wind light to with moderate \ngusts in the afternoon possible.",
